WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.104
Hello and welcome. I'm James Murphy.

00:00:02.104 --> 00:00:03.673
And today, we're talking about pre-commit

00:00:03.673 --> 00:00:07.696
which is a really cool tool that you can use to keep your codebase tidy

00:00:07.696 --> 00:00:12.282
and catch some simple mistakes before things ever get committed into Git.

00:00:12.282 --> 00:00:16.720
It happens. There's a new person on your team that doesn't really know about pep8

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:19.132
or you just forget to run your code formatter

00:00:19.132 --> 00:00:22.057
or you just make a typo in a config file somewhere.

00:00:22.057 --> 00:00:25.750
But at the time, you don't really notice. So, you just go ahead and git commit.

00:00:25.750 --> 00:00:31.458
Then, either something breaks or just the quality of your code base has gone down just a little bit.

00:00:31.458 --> 00:00:34.862
Well, a bad config you can fix and code you can just reformat.

00:00:34.862 --> 00:00:40.080
But still, wouldn't it be better if you could just catch those errors before the commit happens?

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:41.920
That's what pre-commit is for.

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:43.778
Here's the story of pre-commit.

00:00:43.778 --> 00:00:47.149
Basically, git has a notion of what are called commit hooks.

00:00:47.149 --> 00:00:51.342
These are things that happen either before you commit, or just after you commit,

00:00:51.342 --> 00:00:54.148
or when you push, or when you pull, things like that.

00:00:54.148 --> 00:00:59.865
Git will store this and a whole bunch of other information in the dot git folder inside a directory.

00:00:59.865 --> 00:01:04.640
Depending on your editor, this folder might be hidden. So, just look out for that. You might have to unhide it.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:08.202
This hooks folder is where git stores all these different hooks.

00:01:08.202 --> 00:01:10.720
The ones you see here are just samples.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:14.076
If you were to get rid of that dot sample on the end of the file name,

00:01:14.076 --> 00:01:17.568
then git would actually run that action at that corresponding time.

00:01:17.568 --> 00:01:23.280
So, if I have a file named pre-commit, then git will run that script right before a commit happens.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.894
But you don't need to go out and waste your time writing something like this yourself.

00:01:26.894 --> 00:01:29.520
A lot of really good scripts have already been written.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.319
The pre-commit project just makes it really easy

00:01:32.319 --> 00:01:35.724
to pick and choose things that you want to check for your own code base.

00:01:35.724 --> 00:01:36.766
Let's start simple.

00:01:36.766 --> 00:01:41.600
All I want to do is make sure that before I commit anything, I run my code formatter on it.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:44.454
Of course, I can just run the code formatter before I commit.

00:01:44.454 --> 00:01:46.980
But the point is, I shouldn't have to remember to do that.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:48.800
Why not just make it automatic?

00:01:48.800 --> 00:01:50.774
Well, it's super simple to set up.

00:01:50.774 --> 00:01:55.520
Open up your terminal and just pip install pre-commit.

00:01:55.520 --> 00:02:01.840
Then at the root of your project, just make a new file called dot pre-commit-config.yaml.

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:06.152
Then it's just this easy to add automatic code formatting on every commit.

00:02:06.152 --> 00:02:09.340
You specify pre-commit hooks by just giving a git repository.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:14.160
In this case, I'm using Github. But you can actually use any git repository, even a local one.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:16.546
You specify the git tag that it's going to use

00:02:16.546 --> 00:02:21.085
and then some information about what command to run and what arguments to give it.

00:02:21.085 --> 00:02:24.720
In this case, I'll be using black to do the actual code formatting.

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:28.701
Then you use pre-commit to install the git hooks into your git hooks folder.

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:37.680
Now if we go and look in our git hooks folder, we see that we have this new pre-commit file.

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.538
All right. Now let's add our files to git, commit and see what happens.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.536
As you can see, it ran black and the commit failed because some modifications were made

00:02:47.536 --> 00:02:49.976
and it's not going to commit anything automatically.

00:02:49.976 --> 00:02:53.944
I can go and manually inspect the hello.py file and see that everything looks good

00:02:53.944 --> 00:02:56.960
or just compare the diff like I normally would before commit.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:01.796
Note that these modifications to the hello.py file were made after the file was staged.

00:03:01.796 --> 00:03:04.870
That means I need to git add this file again

00:03:04.870 --> 00:03:08.896
or if something went wrong and I don't like the changes that were automatically made,

00:03:08.896 --> 00:03:12.446
then I can git restore the file to go back to the way it was before.

00:03:12.446 --> 00:03:16.880
In this case, I'm happy with the changes. So, I will add and then try to commit again.

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:20.160
This time since everything passed, the commit went through.

00:03:20.160 --> 00:03:24.480
Note that, some graphical editors, like this one PyCharm, have built-in git support

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:28.646
and they will automatically add tracked files when you try to commit.

00:03:28.646 --> 00:03:31.653
So, if that's the case, then it might look more like this.

00:03:31.653 --> 00:03:35.719
Here I have a spurious space in there and I'm going to go ahead and try and commit.

00:03:35.719 --> 00:03:38.416
Then I try to commit and I see the commit failed.

00:03:38.416 --> 00:03:41.169
And I can go to the console to see what errors.

00:03:41.169 --> 00:03:43.066
I see that it deleted the extra space.

00:03:43.066 --> 00:03:47.176
And then I can just go ahead and click commit again and then this time it will succeed.

00:03:47.176 --> 00:03:50.406
So, what else can you do besides automatically formatting your code?

00:03:50.406 --> 00:03:53.520
Well, you can write your own commit hooks. So, you can pretty much do whatever you want.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:57.360
But there are a ton of really good ones already written on this website here.

00:03:57.360 --> 00:03:59.200
Here are just a few of my favorites.

00:03:59.200 --> 00:04:01.252
You can delete trailing white space,

00:04:01.252 --> 00:04:04.225
make sure a file is either empty or ends with a new line,

00:04:04.225 --> 00:04:07.541
make sure that any yaml files have valid yaml in them,

00:04:07.541 --> 00:04:11.564
make sure you're not accidentally committing any really big files into your repository

00:04:11.564 --> 00:04:15.465
and check for any spurious breakpoints that you forgot to delete before committing.

00:04:15.465 --> 00:04:17.978
You can also run your linter like flake8.

00:04:17.978 --> 00:04:21.667
You can reorder and standardize the order that your imports happen in.

00:04:21.667 --> 00:04:25.307
You can check for outdated syntax and replace it with its modern equivalent.

00:04:25.307 --> 00:04:28.382
And this would also be a good place to run your type checker like mypy.

00:04:28.382 --> 00:04:33.120
pre-commit is most popular in the Python world but it's not something that's just limited to Python.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:37.217
There are pre-made scripts for doing things like running Clang-Tidy or Clang-Format,

00:04:37.217 --> 00:04:39.991
or some other code formatter for a different language.

00:04:39.991 --> 00:04:44.480
You don't have to reinstall anything if you change the yaml file. Just go ahead and commit.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:47.421
We see the commit failed because of a mypy error telling us

00:04:47.421 --> 00:04:50.238
that we passed the wrong kind of argument to this function f.

00:04:50.238 --> 00:04:54.207
Also note that, most hooks will only run if an applicable file actually changed.

00:04:54.207 --> 00:04:59.040
So, it's not going to be reformatting code that it already formatted and hasn't changed.

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.261
Finally, there's the question of safety.

00:05:01.261 --> 00:05:06.265
How safe is it to automatically run programs on your source code before committing them?

00:05:06.265 --> 00:05:11.627
Well, this ultimately goes down to the project and whether or not you trust the project to do the right thing.

00:05:11.627 --> 00:05:13.588
Of course, these little scripts can be written by anyone.

00:05:13.588 --> 00:05:16.874
So, if they put something malicious in, then you're going to be out of luck.

00:05:16.874 --> 00:05:21.522
You can fix a specific version or even a specific hash in the config file.

00:05:21.522 --> 00:05:24.595
This helps mitigate things like, what if this project gets hacked

00:05:24.595 --> 00:05:27.164
and an attacker uploads something malicious.

00:05:27.164 --> 00:05:30.240
But as always, only use projects that you really trust.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:35.581
As far as accidental errors go though, like, what if black accidentally changed the meaning of the code.

00:05:35.581 --> 00:05:38.880
Well, that's something that I haven't actually run into.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:43.954
If something did go wrong, it would really be up to you to catch it in the git diff or by running tests.

00:05:43.954 --> 00:05:45.712
Of course, for an accidental mistake,

00:05:45.712 --> 00:05:49.317
you can always revert using the git restore command that I talked about earlier.

00:05:49.317 --> 00:05:51.972
Well, that's all I've got. I hope this makes your repos better.

00:05:51.972 --> 00:05:57.966
Thanks for watching.

